      "id": 1,
      "text": "Nocona was originally called Jordanville, but the name was changed to honor Peta Nocona, a Comanche chief, after a Texas Ranger suggested it.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/nocona-tx",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Gainesville, Henrietta and Western Railway extended its line across the land of pioneer Jordan in 1887, bringing the first railroad to Nocona.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/nocona-tx",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1889 the town launched its first weekly newspaper, and a year later, a bank chartered with $50,000 opened its doors in 1890.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/nocona-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Nocona Boots, founded by Justin, grew into a major oil‑equipment and clothing factory hub that helped the town’s economy expand in the early 20th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
